WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.300
On today's AI briefing, we think the TSMC disclosure is less about anthropic than about the sector.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.440
Every frontier lab carries the same Taiwan exposure,

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.460
which turns compute access into a geopolitical position rather than a procurement line.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.020
This is the AI briefing for Friday, October 30.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:23.920
I'm Michael.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.940
Like everything here, this briefing is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.860
Today's stories share a theme, who controls compute and who gets funded.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:41.140
Anthropic has filed to go public, and the filing names a heavy dependence on TSMC chips made in Taiwan.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.420
Cohere is raising a series E of up to CAD $7 billion.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:53.900
Ottawa is putting in $1.6 billion, released only as jobs and revenue milestones are met.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.600
Mistral has released open weights for a trillion-parameter coding model,

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.540
which could change what security teams pay for.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:08.020
And new funding counts show only 16% of 2024 seed companies have made it past seed.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.840
We'll take the main stories first, then a few quick hits.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.560
Stevens' take on the top of the day.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.520
Cohere has been an amazing story, a company that just keeps finding a way,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.940
a grip on the mountain to cling to, and a wedge to expand.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:31.820
It's easy to be critical of their situation, but I have been a first mover in AI before and rapidly eclipsed by Valley mentality

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.940
Fundraising as a weapon against faster smarter incumbents and in my case

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.660
Naivete about how to play the game as our friend AA puts it

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.220
I like being Canadian

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:51.120
Non-aligned with corporate malfeasance and market affecting behaviors and I am proud to see them moving forward

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.520
Hopefully, this customer strategic investment by countries gets them to break out velocity.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.780
First story, and it's an update on one we've been following for several days.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.700
Anthropics IPO what's new is that the S-1 is filed.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.820
Tis V. Moshowitz noted the filing and don't worry about the vase.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.240
TechMeme reports it discloses heavy reliance on TSMC chips made in Taiwan,

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.440
citing geopolitical risk from tensions with China.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.520
Share price and other financials are not stated.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:28.120
The chip claims are unverified and the full filing hasn't been read.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:29.660
Our read holds.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.020
We think this says more about the sector than about Anthropic.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:41.420
Every frontier lab carries the same Taiwan exposure, so compute access becomes a geopolitical position.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:47.500
For apps built on one lab, a fair question is how they would fail over to a second provider.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.500
The market reading is that a shared risk won't set the IPO apart.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.380
Revenue growth and compute commitments will drive the price.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.720
Here's Stephen's take.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.180
Anthropic might get there and end up driving value like research breakthroughs themselves,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.680
but better code and system models are in whole unlikely to get them to the promised land.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.760
But better and better models are def getting you closer to big,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:21.320
valuable breakthroughs in alliance with scientists, research labs with testing facilities, and

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.800
more, maybe they become the world's biggest pharma company.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.840
Our second story comes from Canada.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:34.380
Cohere has recently opened a Series E, according to BetaKit and Cohere's own announcement.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:41.060
The company is targeting up to $7 billion CAD at a post-money valuation of roughly $36

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:41.480
billion.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.280
Schwarz Group is leading the round.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.960
Inovia Capital and Canada's CDIC are participating.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:53.540
Alongside that, Canada commits $1.6 billion through CDIC.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.240
That money is staged on jobs and revenue milestones.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:04.880
In return, Cohere pledges $1.7 billion in Canadian compute by 2030.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.540
How well does it hold up?

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.320
The core reporting holds.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.180
But the $7 billion is a target.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.180
The size actually closed has not been disclosed.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.720
The government money flows only as milestones are met.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.600
And nothing in the reporting supports the claims about Cohere's competitiveness.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.040
Our view is that the staging matters more than the headline.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.480
Ottawa is buying jobs and domestic compute.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.180
It is not underwriting a $36 billion valuation.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:42.360
valuation. What would justify that price is revenue from private enterprises, outside

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:47.120
government. That looks like the number worth watching.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:53.740
Read through a policy lens. The same facts look different. Public money staged on jobs,

00:04:53.740 --> 00:05:00.960
revenue and $1.7 billion of domestic compute is how a country builds sovereign capacity.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:07.260
On that reading, judging the deal on venture returns alone misses its purpose.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.500
For early-stage companies, the compute pledge could matter.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:16.660
Canadian startups that need domestic hosting might benefit, but whether outside companies

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:20.420
can actually buy that capacity is not stated.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:26.560
That may be a question worth asking before anyone plans around it.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.380
Here's Stephen's take.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:34.020
access to sovereign capability is not unreal. If there was an ally that would not be capable of

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:38.760
using compute as a weapon, they would have less runway but in reality world affairs,

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:44.660
and the third scaling Law Plus access to unique content and experimental capability and

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:49.400
multi-billion dollar national contracts could take them where people like Helsing have gone,

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.940
and others like the entire Silicon Valley emerged in just this way.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.420
Read Steve Blank for example.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:06.120
For our third story, Mistral has released open weights for large-for-its-trillion-parameter model, nicknamed LeChonk.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:11.220
The news comes from Mistral's own announcement, with a report from TechCrunch.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:15.440
Mistral claims it is the best open-weight coding model.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:23.440
Its benchmarks show 19.7% on Frontier Code 2.0 and 41% on Terminal Bench 4.0.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:32.400
Mistral also says it ties GLM 5.3 at 48% while using 47% fewer tokens.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:39.440
TechCrunch reports the model is aimed at cybersecurity work, with more control than closed models offer.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:44.380
How well does it hold up? So far, the picture is mixed.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.460
Those benchmark leads are self-reported and unverified.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:52.480
The claim of being the best goes further than the evidence shows,

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.820
and the cybersecurity angle comes with little detail.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.360
We read this as a pricing event before a capability one.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.380
A one trillion coding model that a company can host itself

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.160
opens room for security and regulated sector startups

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.180
that cannot send their code to a closed API.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.820
For early stage companies, that cuts two ways.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:21.500
Startups whose edge is wrapping closed coding models for security work could lose ground.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:28.500
Startups with fine-tuning and on-prem deployment skills may gain from a credible open-weight base.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.500
A few questions seem worth asking.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:36.500
Do these results hold when someone outside Miestral runs the benchmarks?

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.500
What does the cybersecurity use case look like in practice?

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:44.500
And for a startup building security tools on closed models,

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:49.500
how much of its edge survives if a self-hosted open model is good enough?

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:56.680
Here's Steven's take. Impressive. I guess we can all distill how they built this.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.800
The last of the main stories turns from models to money and to the companies trying to raise it.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.520
In a piece published yesterday, Crunchbase News looks at what large Series A rounds are hiding.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:16.940
In the third quarter, U.S. Series A rounds fell about 28% year-over-year to 272.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.760
Seed rounds dipped to 984 down from 1,059.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:26.360
The median U.S. seed round in 2025 was about $3 million.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:30.260
The striking figures are on graduation.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:37.980
Only 24% of 2023 companies that raised a seed round of $1 million or more have moved past seed.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.780
For 2024, it's 16%.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:44.500
The piece is a guest column by Mark Schroeder of MGV.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:46.900
On the evidence, it's a mixed picture.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:50.860
The crunch-based counts do show a real Series A squeeze.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:56.620
The prescription that founders need to clear a revenue bar is one investor's view.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:01.000
It also doubles as a pitch for MGV's own approach.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.000
And a data company has its own reasons to highlight market stress.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:06.540
Here's where we come out.

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:11.700
We think the 16% graduation rate for 2024 seeds is the number to carry.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:15.820
The Series A bar now looks like revenue quality, not traction.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:22.600
The data supports that much. The prescription is MGVs from a fund pitching its own method.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.900
For seed investors, the implication is practical.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:33.860
With so few companies graduating, seed funds may need larger reserves for extensions and bridge rounds.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:40.580
A fair question for any fund might be how its reserves plan looks against those graduation rates.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:55.320
Here's Stephen's take. I don't know what a Series A is. I just know that this is the gold rush day zero, and people better start laying claims and digging holes fast and supporting those that do fast.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:59.960
Now two quick hits, both about scale.

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:05.860
First, NVIDIA is in talks to give N-Scale billions of dollars in credit guarantees.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:11.700
The debt would fund a 460-megawatt data center that Anthropic plans to use.

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:16.600
It's another sign that chip makers are backstopping the financing of their own demand.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:24.500
Second, Microsoft will offer a voluntary exit to about 60,000 of its 223,000 staff.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:42.260
Azure argues this reflects productivity gains, with revenue per employee up two-thirds since 2022 to $1.5 million. He calls AI the cause in a not-yet sense. Our evidence check finds that argument overstated.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:52.340
On Simon Willison on Spawn's AI-driven game development, here's Stephen's take. To be honest, this is sort of easy now.

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:56.880
Stephen's take on the quick hits.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:02.920
NVIDIA credit guarantees is interesting and important for emerging meta-hyperscalers being

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:08.980
held back by access to compute by debt risk and the potential to use this as yet another

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:12.940
lever against competition.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:18.340
And Stephen's take on the day, or doubles, and what is your plan to separate yourself

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:24.420
from OpenAI disaster, lack of compute as you need it at a predictable price, being able

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:30.660
to offload inference to delayed or training to background. Basically, disaster and change

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:31.140
planning.

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:38.260
To close, a diligence question that might be worth asking this week. What does your gross

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:43.740
margin look like if your model providers price or supply changes by half? And one question

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:50.740
to watch, how much of Cohere's series E actually closed, and what releases Canada's $1.6 billion.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:57.980
A closing announcement, or published CDIC terms with a milestone schedule, would settle it.

