That's the briefing. Stay with me. Right after this, Bella takes you through today's deep dive

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:12.280
on Anthropics leaked AI bill and the fight over preemption. You can also read it or listen to it

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:18.300
on its own on the AI Briefing webpage, where you'll find the written edition and every past episode.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.480
The link is in the show notes.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:31.060
Thanks, Michael. This is the AI Briefing Deep Dive for Friday, October 30, Anthropics Leaked AI Bill

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:38.120
and the Fight Over Preemption. I'm Bella. Like the briefing, it is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:45.900
On October 16, Politico reported that Anthropic had been quietly showing lawmakers on Capitol

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:51.800
Hill a draft federal bill to regulate frontier AI. Politico had the confidential draft.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:56.440
Anthropic has not published it. The reaction has kept building since then.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:01.640
This week, Tizvi Moshowitz, who writes the newsletter Don't Worry About the Vase,

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:04.480
called the leaked draft unacceptable and troubling.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:10.420
California State Senator Scott Weiner said it falls far short of protecting public health and

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:16.520
safety. Anthropic has partly retreated. Its head of federal affairs, Anthony Cimino, said the

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:21.560
preemption wording was not precise enough and would be updated. He also said the proposal is

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:26.600
meant to be the strongest on the table. So this is the moment to ask a structural question.

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:32.300
Suppose a frontier lab helps write the federal rules, and those rules override the states.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.900
Who does that lock in and who gets locked out?

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:41.300
Here is the draft as Politico and other outlets describe it.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:44.700
The Commerce Department would register independent evaluators.

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:49.440
Those evaluators would periodically assess frontier developers and their models.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.980
Developers could pay their own evaluators, as long as the payment is not tied to the result.

00:13:55.740 --> 00:14:00.640
Evaluators would have to tell Commerce within 72 hours if they find an imminent risk.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:04.800
The bill also requires risk reports and whistleblower protections.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.760
ZVI calls its security requirements strong.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:10.920
The fight is over two other parts.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.200
The first is the bar for stopping a model.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:19.640
The government could step in only when a model poses a foreseeable and imminent risk of more than $1 billion in damage,

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:23.040
or of killing or seriously injuring more than 50 people.

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:26.480
The risk would also have to come from a narrow set of scenarios.

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:32.260
Bioweapons, a massive cyberattack, a model evading its developers' safety controls,

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:34.500
or research that speeds those up.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.540
Only a federal judge in Washington could order a suspension.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:40.540
The second part is preemption.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.520
That means a federal law displacing state law.

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:49.920
The draft would override California's SB53 New York's Rays Act and Illinois SB315.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:52.560
One way to picture it is building codes.

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:57.480
Today, states write their own fire codes for one specific kind of tall building.

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.960
Under this draft, Washington writes the only code.

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:03.060
Only Congress can change it.

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:08.280
Inspectors are hired and paid by the builder, and no one licenses them.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:11.280
A city that sees smoke cannot close the building.

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:16.140
It has to ask a federal judge, and the judge must weigh what closing would cost the owner.

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:19.860
That picture also shows what the draft does not appear to cover.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:22.120
It is aimed at frontier developers.

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:25.060
The reporting does not say who counts as one.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:30.080
A seed stage company building on top of a model is probably not the regulated party.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:35.880
It may still feel the effects, through its suppliers and through the market for audits.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.260
Start with what is established.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:42.560
Politico reported the draft from a confidential copy

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:46.360
and from three people familiar with Anthropic's legislative work.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.440
Anthropic has not denied it.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:54.120
Cimino publicly said the company circulated the ideas to get early feedback from policymakers.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:59.400
On October 18, he posted that Anthropic would update its preemption language

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:02.000
to better match its own published framework.

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:06.500
He also said the company would address the standard for blocking models,

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:09.460
risk thresholds, and requirements for evaluators.

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:11.180
So the draft is real.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:14.840
Anthropic stands behind its direction, but not its wording.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:18.360
Several provisions are confirmed by more than one outlet.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:22.880
These include the $1 billion and 50 casualty thresholds,

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:24.660
the federal court in Washington,

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:27.080
the evaluators paid by labs,

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.360
and the list of state laws it would preempt.

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:34.440
Cimino says the harm thresholds were pulled directly from California law.

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:37.700
That holds up, with a catch that Weiner points out.

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:42.000
SB 53 uses those thresholds to trigger disclosure and assessment.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.620
The draft uses them to decide when a model can be stopped.

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:47.600
Next come the legal readings,

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:49.940
which are analysis, not fact.

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:55.160
Charlie Bullock of the Institute for Law and AI says courts must weigh the harm a blocking

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:56.520
order does to the developer.

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:00.940
In his reading, they are not told to weigh the harm of not blocking.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:05.320
Writing in Transformer, he warns of a race to the bottom among auditors.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.480
In this reading, they would compete to offer the least bothersome audits.

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:15.060
Nathan Calvin, as ZVI quotes him, argues that passing the bill would leave us less

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:16.800
protected than existing law does.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:20.120
These are serious readings by people who know the field.

00:17:20.620 --> 00:17:22.280
They are also from safety advocates,

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:25.700
and they are interpretations of a text the public cannot read.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:28.740
Then there is what nobody has shown.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:30.820
The full text is not public.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:34.500
Who counts as covered is not stated in any coverage we found.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.420
Neither is any compute or revenue cutoff.

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:41.960
The revised draft and the promised framework for evaluators have not appeared.

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:46.780
Any claim about how this affects small builders is, for now, inference.

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:50.920
The same facts support several readings.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:55.080
The safety reading, held by ZVI, Calvin, and Wiener,

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:58.160
comes from people who take catastrophic risk seriously.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:02.200
In this view, the draft is weaker than the bipartisan Frontier Act

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.460
from Representatives Obernault and Trahan.

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:07.980
They argue it removes state backstops that work today

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:11.620
and freezes weak federal rules that only Congress can tighten.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:17.320
Zevi's sharpest line is that Anthropic seems more afraid that the government

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:21.220
might enforce rules against Anthropic than worried about the government's power

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:22.980
to enforce rules against everyone.

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:26.100
The market reading is the strongest case for the draft.

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:29.620
A single federal standard is cheaper to comply with than 50.

00:18:30.340 --> 00:18:34.300
Clear thresholds and a single court cut legal uncertainty for every developer.

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:38.580
One outlet reports that child safety, consumer protection,

00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:41.140
and civil rights laws are carved out of preemption.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:45.260
If so, states keep their role on everyday harms.

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:47.400
The realist reading is more skeptical.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:51.920
The scenarios that would justify a shutdown are narrow, and none has happened yet.

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:56.860
On that view, how blocking works matters less than who writes the standards for evaluators

00:18:56.860 --> 00:18:58.000
and who pays for them.

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:01.140
That is where an incumbent's advantage would build up over time.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:04.100
The policy reading points to a shift in roles.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:08.900
Earlier, Anthropic endorsed SB 53 and similar state laws.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:11.140
It is now proposing to replace them.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:13.920
That doesn't prove bad faith.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:18.400
It does mean a lab's policy position is something we should track over time,

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:19.840
not take as fixed.

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:24.100
Our read is that the label of incumbent capture

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:27.040
fits the evaluation market more than it fits the founders.

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:31.520
Frontier-level obligations will almost certainly fall on a few large labs.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:35.300
Most seed and series A companies will not be covered directly.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:37.740
The indirect effects are where it gets real.

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:41.540
One is evaluators who are paid by labs and not licensed.

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:47.560
another is standards that only congress can update a third is state regimes that disappear

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:53.000
together they decide what assurance means for every model your product sits on

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:58.760
for startups selling evaluation red teaming or ai assurance this is close to an existential

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:04.360
question a weak federal floor could create a big market for cheap compliance but not for rigorous

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:10.760
testing for application companies the question is quieter their enterprise customers may now point to

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:16.040
SB53 disclosures from their model providers. Those could vanish or change shape.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:22.420
We hold all of this loosely because the text is not public and a revision has been promised.

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:28.260
One question worth exploring with an assurance startup is this. If the federal standard were

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:33.780
the floor in this draft, would the main buyers still pay for your product? Or does your revenue

00:20:33.780 --> 00:20:40.700
depend on state law that could be preempted? Three things would settle this. First is

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.820
Anthropic's revised preemption language.

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:45.640
Simino promised it, but it has not appeared.

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:48.020
When it does, the test is simple.

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:52.860
Does it still stop states from blocking a model in an emergency, or only from setting

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:54.460
conflicting disclosure rules?

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:56.920
Second is the evaluator framework.

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:02.320
Licensing, conflict of interest rules, and a say for commerce over standards would answer

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:04.000
most of the race-to-the-bottom worry.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:10.680
Third is whether any member of Congress introduces the draft, or folds it into the Frontier Act.

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:15.540
A bill text with a sponsor is the moment this becomes law in the making,

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:17.460
rather than a lobbying document.

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.100
None of these has a public date yet.

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:23.380
Here's Stephen's take.

00:21:23.980 --> 00:21:27.260
Companies becoming governments is a big part of sci-fi lore,

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:29.120
and here we are living in the future.

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:34.040
That's the deep dive and today's AI briefing.

